window.TALK={"title": "Addicted to Clinging", "speaker": "Nomon Tim Burnett", "date": "2026-10-18", "audio": "https://rczc-audio.s3.amazonaws.com/2026/2026-10-18-NomonTimBurnett-AddictedtoClinging.mp4", "duration": 2441, "summary": "In a mid practice period sesshin talk, Nomon Tim Burnett takes up the shuso Juriki's choice to teach the 12 steps as practice and recasts each step in the language of Soto Zen: the power of ignorance and conditioning, turning toward Buddha, Dharma and Sangha, the precepts as a moral inventory, confession and amends, meditation, and the bodhisattva vow. Both paths begin by seeing that the \"I\" who wants to fix things is the problem. The root addiction, Nomon suggests, is clinging to a separate self, which is harder to see than alcohol because we look out through it; Dogen's Genjokoan points to forgetting the self. The talk closes with faith as the ground of practice, reading Shantideva's Training Anthology and its long quotation on faith from the Precious Lamp Dharani Sutra.", "topics": ["12 steps", "recovery", "clinging", "separate self", "precepts", "confession and amends", "Genjokoan", "Shantideva", "Precious Lamp Dharani Sutra", "faith", "sesshin", "bodhisattva vow"], "themes": ["Ethics", "No-self", "Sangha", "Suffering"], "texts": ["Shōbōgenzō"], "figures": ["Buddha", "Dōgen", "Shantideva"], "sentences": [{"p": 0, "t": 4.0, "text": "Well, I hope you're doing well."}, {"p": 0, "t": 6.6, "text": "I have to report I'm having a great time today."}, {"p": 0, "t": 11.1, "text": "This is so fun, just to get to be together quietly with my dharma friends, old and new."}, {"p": 0, "t": 19.9, "text": "You hearing me okay?"}, {"p": 0, "t": 21.7, "text": "Sound okay?"}, {"p": 0, "t": 22.5, "text": "Yeah, great."}, {"p": 0, "t": 23.4, "text": "Sound okay online, Kelly?"}, {"p": 0, "t": 25.2, "text": "Can you hear?"}, {"p": 0, "t": 26.5, "text": "I hope so."}, {"p": 1, "t": 27.8, "text": "I was especially enjoying early this morning."}, {"p": 1, "t": 31.8, "text": "We've been doing this a little while; even if you're fairly new, you're still part of doing this for a little while."}, {"p": 1, "t": 41.5, "text": "It's just so sweet the way people would come in quietly and check on the little jobs list, see what they're supposed to be doing today, think about it for about a second, and then go off and do the thing to get ready and quietly come back."}, {"p": 1, "t": 63.2, "text": "There's a little buzz of activity for sure, but it's so quiet and so grounded."}, {"p": 1, "t": 69.8, "text": "Isn't that nice?"}, {"p": 1, "t": 71.1, "text": "And we get to just be with ourselves and be together."}, {"p": 1, "t": 76.0, "text": "This can also be hard, for sure, right?"}, {"p": 1, "t": 80.4, "text": "Well, what could be better?"}, {"p": 1, "t": 83.5, "text": "Distracting ourselves and avoiding and all the other things?"}, {"p": 1, "t": 88.3, "text": "No."}, {"p": 1, "t": 88.8, "text": "What could be better than getting to just be together with ourselves in sesshin?"}, {"p": 1, "t": 95.0, "text": "And I hope if your experience doesn't match what I'm saying even the least bit, you can just appreciate it however it is for you."}, {"p": 1, "t": 106.0, "text": "I know we're all having different experiences, but I'm the one sitting here with a microphone, so I'm telling you about mine."}, {"p": 1, "t": 117.6, "text": "Later, we'll switch."}, {"p": 2, "t": 119.2, "text": "So mostly I want to reflect today together in our mid-practice period sesshin."}, {"p": 2, "t": 126.6, "text": "In the calendar, a little past mid, mostly because of my schedule."}, {"p": 2, "t": 132.9, "text": "I was down at Norman Fischer's for a week and had to do a retreat, so another retreat."}, {"p": 2, "t": 142.4, "text": "So anyway, end up on this weekend."}, {"p": 2, "t": 146.1, "text": "But I've just been so appreciating our shuso, our head student, Juriki's choice, and kind of a courageous choice (not even kind of, a courageous choice) to bring forward the 12 steps as practice: something that originated in a whole sort of another tradition."}, {"p": 2, "t": 169.8, "text": "But I think Juriki wisely and correctly intuited, well, it's no different from what we do here."}, {"p": 2, "t": 178.4, "text": "It's no different from practice."}, {"p": 2, "t": 180.8, "text": "And he's presented it straight up, as he's experienced it."}, {"p": 2, "t": 187.5, "text": "And the language that he's using to share it includes language like God."}, {"p": 2, "t": 193.7, "text": "I think it says God four or five times in the titles of the 12 steps."}, {"p": 2, "t": 201.4, "text": "And that's unfamiliar to me a little bit."}, {"p": 2, "t": 205.2, "text": "And so I've been appreciating feeling into that kind of language."}, {"p": 3, "t": 211.0, "text": "And I think if somebody had just told me (and maybe probably people have told me), \"Well, 12 steps is no different from Buddha Dharma,\" because that's my experience of it, it's a way of changing and growing and healing and seeing yourself more clearly, understanding how things are."}, {"p": 3, "t": 232.1, "text": "If someone had said that, I'm sure I wouldn't have argued: \"Oh, yeah, okay, great, great.\" But I wouldn't have really known."}, {"p": 3, "t": 242.1, "text": "And I still don't really know."}, {"p": 3, "t": 244.5, "text": "But I have a much deeper feeling that the 12-step world is a world of deep practice, because Juriki didn't just tell me that or tell you that."}, {"p": 3, "t": 257.8, "text": "He showed us, through the example of his own life, through the way he's presenting the 12 steps to us in the Sunday classes, through the way he's living his life, relocated to Bellingham for a couple of months."}, {"p": 3, "t": 271.7, "text": "There are some people you would hesitate to call up and ask them, \"Hey, could you just move up here for a couple of months?\" I did not have any such hesitation in this case."}, {"p": 3, "t": 284.2, "text": "And he said yes right away."}, {"p": 3, "t": 286.3, "text": "Not only he said yes, but other people said yes and came up for two months."}, {"p": 3, "t": 292.0, "text": "Amazing."}, {"p": 4, "t": 292.6, "text": "But anyway, at its root, 12-step is so like Buddhism."}, {"p": 4, "t": 299.3, "text": "It's a response to deep suffering, a path of healing and transformation where the root idea is not \"How do I fix this?\" And whenever we say \"How do I fix this?\" we think we're underlining the word \"fix.\" We're actually underlining the word \"I.\" So both Buddhism and 12-step understand that's not going to work."}, {"p": 4, "t": 334.0, "text": "They understand you've tried that."}, {"p": 4, "t": 337.0, "text": "You've tried I, me, through my willpower and my determination, confident that I understand who I am and what's going on: \"I'm going to fix this.\" No, no, no, it doesn't work."}, {"p": 4, "t": 352.3, "text": "Both systems recognize that the root of the problem isn't something else out there that I can solve."}, {"p": 4, "t": 360.9, "text": "The root of the problem is not really understanding who we are, what this world is."}, {"p": 4, "t": 368.5, "text": "And so the solution is a change in understanding, not a fix."}, {"p": 4, "t": 374.2, "text": "And that change comes through experience, it comes through commitment, it comes through an awful lot of time, a lot of patience."}, {"p": 4, "t": 384.0, "text": "You go through these steps again and again and again, just like we come to practice again and again and again."}, {"p": 5, "t": 392.8, "text": "You might have noticed (I don't know if you noticed this), although there are apparently some sort of stages and steps in practice and stuff like that, we don't have a graduation ceremony."}, {"p": 5, "t": 406.5, "text": "Well, maybe death."}, {"p": 5, "t": 407.8, "text": "There's no graduation, because we're always learning, right?"}, {"p": 5, "t": 411.0, "text": "We're always students of the way."}, {"p": 5, "t": 413.5, "text": "It changes, but we're always deeply students."}, {"p": 6, "t": 416.3, "text": "And so I thought it might be interesting, and hopefully I'm not stealing Sam's thunder (he was planning to do this as an assignment, but I took up an assignment for myself that I want to share with you), to see if I could take the language that we're using in class for the 12 steps and re-express it with the language of the practice that I grew up in, and just see what that feels like."}, {"p": 6, "t": 449.7, "text": "And this is a good thing to do in general."}, {"p": 6, "t": 453.6, "text": "I think sometimes things that are received, we give them a certain kind of helpful power."}, {"p": 6, "t": 460.4, "text": "If we really hear something, there's a helpfulness to really feeling its power."}, {"p": 6, "t": 465.5, "text": "But also there's a sort of rigidity that can be unhelpful."}, {"p": 6, "t": 469.8, "text": "So it's always good to give yourself permission, after you've really taken something in as it is, to play with it a little bit, to write your own version."}, {"p": 6, "t": 481.1, "text": "We do this with our precepts (a big part of our way of doing the 12 steps is through the precepts)."}, {"p": 6, "t": 489.1, "text": "We invite people to write their own versions of the bodhisattva precepts."}, {"p": 6, "t": 493.3, "text": "Make them your own."}, {"p": 6, "t": 494.7, "text": "So here's my attempt at this."}, {"p": 6, "t": 496.8, "text": "See what you think."}, {"p": 7, "t": 498.2, "text": "Step one in the original: *Admitted that we were powerless over alcohol and that our lives had become unmanageable.* Admitted that we were powerless over alcohol and our lives had become unmanageable."}, {"p": 7, "t": 517.3, "text": "Maybe: admitted the incredible power of ignorance and conditioning (this one I had to kind of...), and our lives lived in that way are inherently unsatisfactory and painful."}, {"p": 7, "t": 535.2, "text": "I'm still working on this one."}, {"p": 7, "t": 539.0, "text": "But to admit the incredible power of ignorance and conditioning, that our lives lived in the shadow of that are inherently unsatisfactory and painful."}, {"p": 7, "t": 549.2, "text": "No matter what you do, right?"}, {"p": 7, "t": 551.8, "text": "No matter what you do."}, {"p": 8, "t": 553.9, "text": "So both of these drop a lot out, right?"}, {"p": 8, "t": 557.8, "text": "They drop out our sureness about who and what we are: \"I alone can fix this or solve this.\" They recognize that what we're working with is much bigger than ourselves, and there's something that we're not getting about it."}, {"p": 8, "t": 575.2, "text": "If we feel powerless in the face of alcohol, or lost in a life of constant whirling and twirling, inherently unsatisfactory, never quite getting to what we want, we have to really bring up a lot of humility, don't we?"}, {"p": 8, "t": 593.7, "text": "And a lot of letting go."}, {"p": 8, "t": 596.5, "text": "And a lot of making space for something else."}, {"p": 9, "t": 600.7, "text": "So then step two, original: *Come to believe that a power greater than ourselves could restore us to sanity.* This one's pretty close."}, {"p": 9, "t": 612.6, "text": "Come to understand that a way greater than ourselves could restore us to sanity."}, {"p": 9, "t": 620.1, "text": "So the way, the power, something beyond this idea of me is needed here."}, {"p": 9, "t": 627.5, "text": "And Juriki was also very generous in telling us his story of the me being knocked down again and again and again."}, {"p": 9, "t": 638.1, "text": "Last time I talked about this, I gave him a hard time for how many times he said, \"And then the hardest thing in my life happened.\" How could that keep happening?"}, {"p": 9, "t": 650.3, "text": "But it did."}, {"p": 10, "t": 651.4, "text": "And it's tricky."}, {"p": 10, "t": 653.3, "text": "We do have this perception of self and the things we're learning and doing."}, {"p": 10, "t": 659.2, "text": "And it's not wrong exactly, but it's not right either."}, {"p": 10, "t": 663.8, "text": "And I thought in all of the areas where his story was shared with us, and your stories have been lived, it does seem that when there's real change and growth, there's something bigger happening."}, {"p": 10, "t": 679.9, "text": "It's not something you figured out."}, {"p": 10, "t": 682.7, "text": "It's something bigger than that."}, {"p": 10, "t": 685.0, "text": "So is it a power?"}, {"p": 10, "t": 686.9, "text": "Is it a way?"}, {"p": 10, "t": 688.4, "text": "Is it Buddha?"}, {"p": 10, "t": 689.6, "text": "And the problem with all of this, when we talk about it, is we only have our words and concepts, right, which are very crude."}, {"p": 10, "t": 699.8, "text": "This is subtle territory."}, {"p": 10, "t": 701.3, "text": "So we recognize that we don't understand."}, {"p": 10, "t": 704.0, "text": "We recognize we need something bigger than us."}, {"p": 11, "t": 707.0, "text": "And then step three: we've got to commit to it."}, {"p": 11, "t": 711.6, "text": "So it's pretty logical actually, the twelve steps, just like practice."}, {"p": 11, "t": 716.7, "text": "Step three in the original: *Make a decision to turn our will and our life over the care of God as we understand him.* My version: make a decision to turn our will and our life over to Buddha, Dharma, and Sangha."}, {"p": 11, "t": 736.0, "text": "To the way of awakening, the teachings, the community, much bigger than me, so much bigger than me."}, {"p": 12, "t": 744.7, "text": "And when studying the Buddha, Dharma, and Sangha, and also I'm sure in studying how to turn your life over to care of God, we recognize that we are a part of this."}, {"p": 12, "t": 760.8, "text": "It's not something over there that we will now join."}, {"p": 12, "t": 765.6, "text": "It's a remembering, right?"}, {"p": 12, "t": 767.5, "text": "Remembering like returning."}, {"p": 12, "t": 769.0, "text": "Remembering literally means embodiment, right?"}, {"p": 12, "t": 771.5, "text": "Remembering: these are our members."}, {"p": 12, "t": 774.1, "text": "That we are Buddha, that we are God, that we are ancestors."}, {"p": 12, "t": 780.2, "text": "We were reciting and venerating our ancestors this morning in our morning service."}, {"p": 12, "t": 786.8, "text": "So remembering that we have been ancestors all along, that's bigger than me."}, {"p": 12, "t": 793.4, "text": "And so this commitment isn't like this."}, {"p": 12, "t": 797.0, "text": "It takes effort, right?"}, {"p": 12, "t": 799.2, "text": "It's hard, but it's not the kind of commitment that's me doing something, right?"}, {"p": 12, "t": 807.1, "text": "It's also emerging, and a letting go, a softening, but with purpose, right, with direction."}, {"p": 13, "t": 815.5, "text": "Okay, so we've recognized the problem is bigger than self."}, {"p": 13, "t": 821.1, "text": "We need to turn towards that vastness, and we've got to commit to it."}, {"p": 13, "t": 829.0, "text": "Step four is like, well, let's get to work."}, {"p": 13, "t": 833.9, "text": "What does it actually look like on the ground?"}, {"p": 13, "t": 838.9, "text": "And the first suggestion in step four: *Make a searching and fearless moral inventory of ourselves.* Maybe: study and practice the precepts, to see and understand our skillful and unskillful actions of body, speech, and mind."}, {"p": 13, "t": 858.6, "text": "Study and practice precepts to see and understand our skillful and unskillful actions of body, speech, and mind."}, {"p": 14, "t": 867.4, "text": "In both of these, I think it's not just a list, right?"}, {"p": 14, "t": 872.7, "text": "It's understanding the being experience that led to all those things happening, right?"}, {"p": 14, "t": 878.4, "text": "It's like following a chain of evidence back."}, {"p": 14, "t": 882.8, "text": "It's not our opinions; it's seeing what we actually do, and wondering what's happening with this being that does these things."}, {"p": 14, "t": 892.0, "text": "So you're looking at yourself a little bit from the perspective of God and Buddha, even though it is all you, and you are the one who is responsible for doing something about it."}, {"p": 14, "t": 906.5, "text": "But it's not exactly your fault, right?"}, {"p": 14, "t": 909.5, "text": "You got here honestly, through all the forces that formed your conditioned being."}, {"p": 14, "t": 915.0, "text": "So that's step four."}, {"p": 14, "t": 916.6, "text": "We start getting real about what's happening."}, {"p": 15, "t": 919.5, "text": "And step five is just a continuation of that."}, {"p": 15, "t": 923.2, "text": "Go deeper, go deeper with this."}, {"p": 15, "t": 925.6, "text": "Original language: *Admitted to God, to ourselves, [unclear: \"being\"] the exact nature of our wrongs.* You can't just do this on your own, right?"}, {"p": 15, "t": 935.0, "text": "No question there."}, {"p": 15, "t": 936.7, "text": "My version: admitted to Buddha, to our teachers, to ourselves, to trusted others (maybe right here), the exact nature of our confusion, right?"}, {"p": 15, "t": 949.7, "text": "Our confusion, our wrongs."}, {"p": 16, "t": 952.0, "text": "So this is the pure practice of confession, which we do in a generic way, a general way, almost every time we do a ritual: we avow our ancient twisted karma."}, {"p": 16, "t": 963.4, "text": "Maybe there's a little internal effort we should be doing with that kind of practice of, well, what is this really for me?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 971.3, "text": "This is avowing my impatience, my judgment, right?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 974.7, "text": "My narrowness, my righteousness."}, {"p": 16, "t": 976.9, "text": "Letting it go."}, {"p": 16, "t": 978.6, "text": "So four and five, I think, are two sides of the same coin, right?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 986.3, "text": "Really being real about our conditioned selves, and feeling our conditioned selves in the context of the unconditioned, right?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 995.1, "text": "In the context of Buddha, in the context of God."}, {"p": 16, "t": 998.1, "text": "Because then there's room, right?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 999.6, "text": "Then there's infinite space."}, {"p": 16, "t": 1000.8, "text": "And it's only me and mine, and \"How do I fix this?\" It's very constricted, right?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 1005.6, "text": "It's very small."}, {"p": 16, "t": 1006.5, "text": "There's no room for anything."}, {"p": 16, "t": 1008.0, "text": "No wonder maybe we seem to succeed for a little while, but then we fall right back on our butts, right?"}, {"p": 16, "t": 1018.6, "text": "There's no space."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1020.1, "text": "And then I don't know what the originals are, but the 12 steps that Juriki gave us, combining some things next, they combine steps six and seven with this statement: *We're entirely ready to have God remove all these defects of character."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1041.9, "text": "Humbly asked him to remove our shortcomings.* It doesn't get more [unclear: \"bigger in themselves\"] than that."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1051.1, "text": "I can't do it."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1053.4, "text": "God can do it."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1055.7, "text": "But maybe in this context, something like: choose to devote ourselves fully to the practice with faith in the way of Buddha, willingness to enter the path of Dharma, humbly devoted to Sangha life."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1074.9, "text": "So I'm a little more wordy, I guess."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1078.8, "text": "Faith in the way of Buddha, willingness, the path, the teachings of Dharma, humbly devoting ourselves to Sangha life."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1088.2, "text": "So this step is almost all shifting the context, right?"}, {"p": 17, "t": 1093.6, "text": "We're making a choice, but we're shifting the context."}, {"p": 17, "t": 1098.0, "text": "It's not just my life anymore, right?"}, {"p": 17, "t": 1101.5, "text": "God's life, Buddha's life, lived with Dharma and Sangha, lived with God as we understand him or her or them."}, {"p": 18, "t": 1111.5, "text": "And then steps eight, nine, and ten are also mixed, combined together."}, {"p": 18, "t": 1117.5, "text": "So I'm sure it just somehow made sense."}, {"p": 18, "t": 1122.5, "text": "I don't know what the originals are."}, {"p": 18, "t": 1126.0, "text": "But eight, nine, and ten that we received from the 12-step tradition: *Make a list of all persons we have harmed and became willing to make amends to them all, made direct amends to such people wherever possible, except when to do so would injure them or others, continued to take personal inventory, and when we were wrong, promptly admitted it.*"}, {"p": 19, "t": 1157.0, "text": "So this is very thoroughgoing."}, {"p": 19, "t": 1159.9, "text": "So: seeking to right the wrongs."}, {"p": 19, "t": 1163.7, "text": "Getting out there, making amends, and being smart about it, right?"}, {"p": 19, "t": 1169.0, "text": "There's some intelligence in this step."}, {"p": 19, "t": 1172.8, "text": "Discerning: sometimes making amends is internal, and you don't bother the person anymore."}, {"p": 19, "t": 1179.1, "text": "You've caused enough damage."}, {"p": 19, "t": 1181.0, "text": "You're just setting them up and saying, \"Look, I'm really sorry for what I did back then."}, {"p": 19, "t": 1190.9, "text": "Really sorry.\" Maybe Buddhist language, something like: understood our unskillful actions as violations of precepts, made amends to those who were harmed when possible and appropriate, and continued, continued to practice precepts, with the guidance of teacher and sangha, with humility and dedication."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1213.2, "text": "So the second part is really important."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1216.2, "text": "It's not a one-time deal, right?"}, {"p": 20, "t": 1219.1, "text": "This is the practice of a lifetime in both systems."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1223.3, "text": "Continued to take personal inventory, when we were wrong admitting it."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1228.0, "text": "Continuing to practice with precepts."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1229.8, "text": "That's one of the things that actually really surprised me and was quite illuminating, some of the first times I ever met somebody in recovery: people in recovery, at least I think the way this is understood, they never say they graduated either."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1245.5, "text": "They never say, \"Oh, I used to be an addict, but I solved that way back.\" We have a member who was addicted to alcohol in his 20s, early 30s, who's now close to 80."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1259.2, "text": "If you ask him, he'll say, \"Yeah, I'm in recovery."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1263.0, "text": "I'm in recovery.\" They might share how many decades it's been since they've used that substance, but they're still in recovery."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1271.0, "text": "They're still practicing."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1272.2, "text": "So precepts are like that too, right?"}, {"p": 20, "t": 1275.1, "text": "It's a lifetime thing."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1276.8, "text": "So there's a sense of continuity of action, continuity of practice, in both of these systems."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1283.4, "text": "I think it's so beautiful and so powerful and so important, because it's another sort of antidote to our dualistic thinking."}, {"p": 20, "t": 1295.2, "text": "Here's the problem with thinking something's broken: then you can also mistakenly think that it's fixed, right?"}, {"p": 20, "t": 1304.2, "text": "And neither is true, not broken or fixed."}, {"p": 21, "t": 1308.4, "text": "Almost done here with this."}, {"p": 21, "t": 1311.1, "text": "So we've gone this far."}, {"p": 21, "t": 1313.8, "text": "Now step 11: *Sought through prayer and meditation to improve our conscious contact with God as we understand him, praying only for knowledge of his will and for us, his will for us and the power to carry that out.* And this, in Christian fashion, has capital H for the him and his."}, {"p": 21, "t": 1333.8, "text": "*Sought through prayer and meditation to improve our conscious contact with God as we understand him, praying only for the knowledge of his will and the power to carry that out.* Here, meditation actually gets mentioned, so that's cool."}, {"p": 21, "t": 1352.2, "text": "Maybe we might say: practice meditation deeply and continuously to understand our own conditioning."}, {"p": 21, "t": 1359.7, "text": "Study the Buddha Dharma to understand the nature of suffering, the causes of suffering, and the path of freedom, right?"}, {"p": 21, "t": 1370.6, "text": "So the four noble truths for us are God's will, God's will for us: to practice the path."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1381.0, "text": "And again, both these systems have this deep, deep acknowledgement: we're not hiding, we can't hide anymore."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1388.0, "text": "We're not hiding from our suffering, even if our suffering is very subtle, very quiet."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1394.9, "text": "Maybe I shared this before, but another thing that happened after Juriki shared his life story was somebody came to dokusan and said, \"I'm really embarrassed that I have really happy parents and great childhood and no real problems, and what am I doing here?\" So we had to explore that a little bit right now."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1417.2, "text": "Well, actually, it's different, just different forms for practice."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1422.5, "text": "Maybe some are relatively less difficult; I don't know if that's true or not."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1429.5, "text": "Anyway, it was interesting."}, {"p": 22, "t": 1431.5, "text": "I was a little bit embarrassed, not to be suffering enough."}, {"p": 23, "t": 1438.0, "text": "Last step, step 12: *Having had a spiritual awakening as a result of these steps, we tried to carry this message to alcoholics and to practice these principles in all our affairs.* Having had a spiritual awakening as a result of these steps (which could be a decade or more of practice, right?), as a result of these steps, we tried to carry this message to alcoholics and to practice these principles in all our affairs."}, {"p": 23, "t": 1470.4, "text": "So here's the bodhisattva vision, right, clear as day."}, {"p": 23, "t": 1473.6, "text": "Having realized the true nature of being, committed to lives as bodhisattvas devoted to saving all beings."}, {"p": 23, "t": 1480.8, "text": "We have some useful language here that maybe 12-step didn't have access to, like bodhisattva, being committed to the awakening of all beings."}, {"p": 23, "t": 1491.7, "text": "And awakening not through cheering ourselves up, right?"}, {"p": 23, "t": 1496.0, "text": "Awakening through deep, deep understanding, wisdom and compassion meeting perfectly."}, {"p": 24, "t": 1500.6, "text": "Having realized this, we recognize our commitment."}, {"p": 24, "t": 1503.8, "text": "And so it's so beautiful to see this step in the original, too: that we're not like, \"Oh, thanks, guys, I'm all done now.\" Then you become, I guess, a sponsor, and keep going to meetings and support other people and share, whenever appropriate, how helpful this process could be."}, {"p": 24, "t": 1525.1, "text": "So it's the same for us."}, {"p": 24, "t": 1528.2, "text": "And we don't run around saying, \"Hey, Zen practice is the best thing ever, you should come immediately.\" But if somebody asks, we share, and we're glad if they're interested."}, {"p": 24, "t": 1542.8, "text": "Maybe this particular form of practice, but it's here, and we're open to all, because that's our commitment."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1552.3, "text": "But one of the things here that can be harder to see in this context is (and I'm sure it's more complicated than this; this would be simplifying), alcohol can be seen with your eyes, right, and tasted with your tongue and felt in your belly and your mind as it affects you."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1578.5, "text": "You can know if you're drinking or not drinking."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1582.5, "text": "But our root addiction is not something separate like that."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1587.0, "text": "Our root illness, our root confusion, our root not-quite-rightness is our very sense of who and what we are, right?"}, {"p": 25, "t": 1595.9, "text": "Our belief in our separateness, our belief in all that conditioning."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1600.4, "text": "It's important to know about that conditioning, but then the trap is we start to really believe it."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1608.6, "text": "And then we're limited by it: our me-ness, me-ness, our separateness, and how this me is kind of like its own little thing, and we've got to spend the rest of our lives protecting it, because it's pretty threatenable, pretty fragile, really."}, {"p": 25, "t": 1630.2, "text": "And we have to carry it around all the time."}, {"p": 26, "t": 1635.0, "text": "And maybe our conditioning is the kind of shadow side of that: we think our me is actually really terrible, really horrible and shameful."}, {"p": 26, "t": 1642.6, "text": "So not only do we have to carry it around all the time, we have to hide it all the time."}, {"p": 26, "t": 1648.7, "text": "What a burden."}, {"p": 26, "t": 1650.2, "text": "Pretend to be some better version, better me, that other people might like."}, {"p": 26, "t": 1654.4, "text": "There's a dishonesty in all of this, right?"}, {"p": 26, "t": 1657.4, "text": "Sometimes we talk about the various social conceits, which smooth the wheels and stuff, just being friendly and nice and, \"Hey, how you doing?\" I don't really mean it, I'm just saying that, and blah, blah, blah."}, {"p": 26, "t": 1673.2, "text": "But there's a dishonesty in all that, right?"}, {"p": 26, "t": 1676.5, "text": "That pains us all."}, {"p": 27, "t": 1678.1, "text": "So it's tricky, I think, about the path here: it's hard to even see the root of the problem."}, {"p": 27, "t": 1686.2, "text": "And I suppose maybe there's more true in 12-step in the exact same way than I understand yet."}, {"p": 27, "t": 1693.7, "text": "It does seem like alcohol is a little more apparent than belief in separate self, because we're so in the middle of ourself, and it's what we operate with and think through and think with, that you can't really see yourself, right?"}, {"p": 27, "t": 1710.1, "text": "The eyeball can't see itself, right?"}, {"p": 27, "t": 1712.5, "text": "So our practice is more of a felt thing, isn't it, than a thought thing or a seen thing."}, {"p": 27, "t": 1719.9, "text": "Little by little we start to feel that this separateness is a delusion, is an illusion."}, {"p": 27, "t": 1726.5, "text": "Maybe sort of vaguely useful in a utility kind of way, but also a prison."}, {"p": 27, "t": 1734.0, "text": "And we don't need it."}, {"p": 28, "t": 1736.5, "text": "I'm so delighted this morning happened to be the morning that we read, recited, Dogen's Genjokoan teaching."}, {"p": 28, "t": 1745.1, "text": "So maybe this sounds familiar from that."}, {"p": 28, "t": 1749.3, "text": "Dogen is always whispering in our ears."}, {"p": 28, "t": 1753.0, "text": "And there are so many treasures in that, but this one in particular, I think:"}, {"p": 29, "t": 1760.9, "text": "*To study the Buddha way is to study the self."}, {"p": 29, "t": 1766.1, "text": "To study the self is to forget the self.*"}, {"p": 30, "t": 1770.7, "text": "To forget the self."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1772.8, "text": "That's it, right?"}, {"p": 30, "t": 1774.3, "text": "*To forget the self is to be actualized by myriad things.* I think the language in our translation is something like that."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1784.0, "text": "*To forget the self is to be actualized by myriad things.* I was thinking about this, and the words \"actualized\" and \"myriad\" are both a little clunky."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1794.7, "text": "We don't run around talking about actualized or myriad."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1800.1, "text": "Maybe something like: to forget the self, or to soften our grip on the self, is to wake up."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1810.1, "text": "To wake up with everything and everyone."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1813.8, "text": "That's myriad, right?"}, {"p": 30, "t": 1815.4, "text": "Everything and everyone."}, {"p": 30, "t": 1817.0, "text": "Continuously and infinitely."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1818.4, "text": "And it all boils down to clinging, right?"}, {"p": 31, "t": 1822.2, "text": "One way or the other."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1825.6, "text": "Clinging to this idea, that idea, and the ideas about who we are are the ones we cling to the most tightly, without always even knowing that we are clinging to them, because it seems true: \"I am this kind of person.\" And in the end, it's not actually so mystical exactly, although everything is mystical."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1858.0, "text": "We do this through experience, right?"}, {"p": 31, "t": 1862.0, "text": "We didn't just read about sesshin."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1866.8, "text": "There's some wonderful things about sesshin, books and essays."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1870.2, "text": "Gary Snyder has an early essay of a sesshin he went to at a monastery in Japan."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1876.7, "text": "It's just beautiful."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1877.8, "text": "Very moving to read that essay."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1880.1, "text": "But we are not reading an essay."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1883.0, "text": "We are doing sesshin."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1884.9, "text": "We are doing the practice."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1887.3, "text": "We are experiencing ourselves."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1889.3, "text": "Experiencing ourselves."}, {"p": 31, "t": 1890.3, "text": "We are experiencing ourselves, experiencing ourselves."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1893.2, "text": "And we all have experiences when we are in our own way."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1899.0, "text": "We all have experiences when we get out of our way."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1903.3, "text": "We all have experiences when the clinging to self softens and loosens and we are open."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1909.5, "text": "And we all have experiences where we constrict."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1912.7, "text": "So that's our true teacher here."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1915.0, "text": "But luckily, we have teachings to help us navigate."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1919.4, "text": "Otherwise, we are just lost in clinging, unclinging, clinging, unclinging, which is all clinging in the end."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1927.7, "text": "And Dogen, like all the great Buddhist teachers, is really encouraging us not to settle for feeling a little bit better."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1937.6, "text": "Nothing wrong with feeling better, but not to settle for that."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1942.3, "text": "To have a bigger vision."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1944.4, "text": "Twelve steps too, right?"}, {"p": 32, "t": 1946.1, "text": "That's not just harm reduction, although harm reduction is an important part of it."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1952.1, "text": "It's freedom."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1953.0, "text": "Like freedom from addiction."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1955.7, "text": "To be fully in recovery."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1959.1, "text": "To be free."}, {"p": 32, "t": 1961.1, "text": "For us, complete freedom from clinging is the vision."}, {"p": 33, "t": 1967.2, "text": "And so I want to talk a little bit about something connected, and then I'll close."}, {"p": 33, "t": 1978.0, "text": "Hopefully I won't go too much past the time we put on the schedule."}, {"p": 33, "t": 1982.8, "text": "Because I can't help it: the way I think, and the way many of us think, is it sounds like techniques and stuff to remember and stuff to do, and go have the sesshin and do these steps and blah blah."}, {"p": 33, "t": 1998.6, "text": "I think there's a deeper commonality and a deeper root in all these systems that doesn't get talked about as much."}, {"p": 33, "t": 2009.6, "text": "It's implied."}, {"p": 33, "t": 2010.7, "text": "And that is the quality of faith, or trust, which isn't exactly tangible or cognitive or sensible even."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2020.3, "text": "I want to close by sharing some teachings on faith that are not from the Christian tradition actually, but straight from Buddhism."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2032.4, "text": "Some of us have studied a little bit about the 8th-century Indian Buddhist teacher Shantideva, who [unclear: \"was a famous poem\"], *The Guide to the Bodhisattva Life*."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2045.1, "text": "He also is credited with another work, which is a much longer work, great big thick book in English translation, that might be translated as *The Training Anthology of Shantideva*."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2057.1, "text": "It's kind of like a manual of practice."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2061.6, "text": "He wrote an inspiring poem of practice, and then, okay, here's the manual."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2069.5, "text": "Thousands of Sanskrit verses translated into English; it's four or five hundred pages."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2078.0, "text": "And I haven't gotten that far in it, because it's really long, but I'm into it."}, {"p": 34, "t": 2084.0, "text": "Little by little."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2085.1, "text": "And what I really want to share just now is where he starts."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2089.9, "text": "He's going to lay out this vast path of practice."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2094.4, "text": "He thought about this a lot, clearly."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2097.0, "text": "And where does he start?"}, {"p": 35, "t": 2100.2, "text": "Faith."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2100.8, "text": "Faith."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2101.5, "text": "Let me just share a little bit from Shantideva."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2107.2, "text": "On behalf of yourself and all beings, please reflect."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2112.9, "text": "First he's going to have a little verse, and then he's going to have a long quotation about faith."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2122.8, "text": "Here's the verse."}, {"p": 35, "t": 2124.1, "text": "This is his verse:"}, {"p": 36, "t": 2125.8, "text": "*Someone who wishes to end suffering and go to the utmost limits of happiness should securely plant the root of faith and fix the mind unwaveringly on awakening.*"}, {"p": 37, "t": 2139.1, "text": "*Someone who wishes to end suffering and go to the ultimate limits of happiness should securely plant the root of faith.*"}, {"p": 38, "t": 2151.0, "text": "I like that language: plant the root of faith."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2155.0, "text": "Fix the mind unwaveringly on awakening."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2157.6, "text": "Then he has a long quotation from a sutra."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2161.6, "text": "He quotes like sixty, seventy sutras about this thing, so he was a well-read guy."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2168.7, "text": "This one's called the *Precious Lamp Dharani Sutra*, which I hadn't heard of before."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2174.0, "text": "So there's a little repetition here, so just settle into it, and I will try to restrain from commenting."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2180.7, "text": "I just want to share this vision of faith."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2184.0, "text": "One sort of presage I want to make: it does use the language \"victorious ones\" to talk about people who've gone far in their practice."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2193.9, "text": "Victorious ones."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2194.6, "text": "They use the language of victory and war a bit in these early teachings: your defilements are the enemies, and you overcome them."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2203.2, "text": "You're victorious."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2204.0, "text": "Just the language they use."}, {"p": 38, "t": 2206.0, "text": "Okay, so here's from the *Precious Lamp Dharani Sutra*:"}, {"p": 39, "t": 2209.5, "text": "*Because of having faith in the victorious ones and their teachings, and by having faith in the practice of the Buddha's heirs, because of having faith in the highest awakening, the mind of a great person is born."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2225.6, "text": "Faith coming first is the mother, giving birth to all abilities, protecting them, helping them grow."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2233.9, "text": "Faith [unclear: \"thrives\"] away doubts and carries you over the flood."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2238.9, "text": "Faith shows you the city of peace."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2242.2, "text": "Faith makes the mind pure and clear."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2245.5, "text": "Free from concept and conceit is the root of respect."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2250.2, "text": "Faith is the root of respect."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2253.0, "text": "Faith is the storehouse of wealth, is your best [unclear: \"foot\"]."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2257.4, "text": "Like a hand is the source of all good karma."}, {"p": 39, "t": 2261.9, "text": "Faith leads to joy and giving and brings delight in the teachings of the victorious ones.*"}, {"p": 40, "t": 2269.0, "text": "*Faith makes you distinguished in abilities and pristine awareness."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2273.0, "text": "Shows you the state of a Buddha and helps you attain it."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2278.3, "text": "Faith clarifies your faculties and makes them sharper."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2281.9, "text": "The power of faith cannot be overcome."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2285.0, "text": "Is the basis of eliminating the reactive emotions."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2289.7, "text": "Faith seeks out your natural abilities, for those who have faith are not attached to the pleasures of contact."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2301.0, "text": "Faith wards off unfavorable circumstances and is itself the single best opportunity."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2310.1, "text": "Faith shows you the highest path of freedom."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2316.2, "text": "Faith is the unspoiled seed, the cause of abilities."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2323.0, "text": "Faith grows into the tree of awakening."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2326.6, "text": "Increases the special bliss of pristine awareness."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2330.2, "text": "Faith shows you all the victorious ones,* all the awakened ones."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2335.9, "text": "*Those who have faith and devotion to the Buddha don't give up on moral discipline and their training."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2344.4, "text": "And those who don't give up on moral discipline and their training are praised as having abilities above all others."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2353.3, "text": "Having inconceivable confidence in Dharma, those who have faith and devotion to the spiritual community will have irreversible confidence."}, {"p": 40, "t": 2363.2, "text": "And having irreversible confidence becomes the irreversible power of faith.*"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2368.7, "text": "And it goes on like that for quite some time."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2373.5, "text": "So let's play with that today."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2376.4, "text": "We're so evaluative, and more than a little judgy, right?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2380.0, "text": "Is this working for me?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2381.8, "text": "Am I doing it right?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2383.6, "text": "Am I doing it wrong?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2385.5, "text": "Is so-and-so doing it right or wrong?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2388.6, "text": "Admiring, and maybe not so much admiring, other people."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2391.4, "text": "Do I want to be here?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2393.2, "text": "What time is it?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2394.5, "text": "How long is this going to go on?"}, {"p": 41, "t": 2396.9, "text": "Blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2398.8, "text": "We can just go back to faith."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2401.5, "text": "Faith in the breath."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2402.8, "text": "Faith in this moment."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2404.0, "text": "Faith in sunlight."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2405.6, "text": "Faith in each other."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2407.7, "text": "Faith in practice."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2409.3, "text": "Faith that although it is really true that we are deeply conditioned in a million, zillion ways (all kind of addictive stuff going on), faith that we are also free."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2424.2, "text": "Faith that we are also Buddha."}, {"p": 41, "t": 2426.6, "text": "Faith that we are also God. [unclear: \"Tension, the equally extent.\"]"}], "verbatim": [{"t": 4, "text": "Well, I hope you're doing well. I have to report I'm having a great time today. This is so fun, just to get to be together quietly with my dharma friends, you know, old and new. You hearing me okay? Sound okay? Yeah, great. Sound okay online, Kelly? Can you hear? I hope so. I was especially enjoying early this morning. You know, we've been doing this a little while, even if you're fairly new, you're still part of doing this for a little while. It's just so sweet the way people would come in quietly and check on the little jobs list, see what they're supposed to be doing today and think about it for about a second and then go off and do the thing to get ready and quietly come back. There's a little buzz of activity for sure, but it's so quiet and so grounded. Isn't that nice? And we get to just be with ourselves and be together. This can also be hard, you know, for sure, right? Well, what could be better, you know? Distracting ourselves and avoiding and all the other things, you know? No. What could be better than getting to just be together with ourselves in sesshin? And I hope if your experience doesn't match what I'm saying even the least bit, you can just appreciate it however it is for you."}, {"t": 106, "text": "I know we're all having different experiences, but I'm the one sitting here with a microphone, so I'm telling you about mine. Later, we'll switch. So mostly I want to reflect today together in our mid-practice period sesshin. In the calendar, a little past mid, mostly because of my schedule. I was down in Norman Fischer's for a week and had to do a retreat, so another retreat. So anyway, end up on this weekend. But I've just been so appreciating our shuso, our head student, Juriki's choice, and kind of a courageous choice. Not even kind of. A courageous choice to bring forward the 12 steps as practice."}, {"t": 164, "text": "Something that originated in a whole sort of another tradition, you know? But I think Juriki wisely and correctly intuited, like, well, it's no different from what we do here. It's no different from practice. And he's presented it, you know, straight up, you know, as he's experienced it. And the language that he's using to share it includes language like God. I think it says God four or five times in the titles of the 12 steps. And that's unfamiliar to me a little bit. And so I've been appreciating feeling into those, that kind of language."}, {"t": 211, "text": "And I think if somebody had just told me, and maybe probably people have told me, well, you know, 12 steps is no different from Buddha Dharma because, you know, that's my experience of it. It's a way of changing and growing and healing and seeing yourself more clearly, understanding how things are. If someone had said that, I'm sure I wouldn't have argued, you know, like, oh, yeah, okay, great, great. But I wouldn't have really known. And I still don't really know. But I have a much deeper feeling that the 12-step world is a world of deep practice. Because Juriki didn't just tell me that or tell you that."}, {"t": 256, "text": "He showed us, you know, he showed us through the example of his own life, through the way he's presenting the 12 steps to us in the Sunday classes, through the way he's living his life relocated to Bellingham for a couple of months. There are some people you would hesitate to call up and ask them, hey, could you just move up here for a couple of months? I did not have any such hesitation in this case. And he said yes right away. Not only he said yes, but other people said yes and came up for two months."}, {"t": 292, "text": "Amazing. But anyway, at its root, 12-step is so like Buddhism. It's a response to deep suffering, a path of healing and transformation where the root idea is not how do I fix this? And whenever we say how do I fix this, we think we're underlining the word fix. We're actually underlining the word I. So both Buddhism and 12-step understand that's not going to work. They understand, you've tried that."}, {"t": 337, "text": "You've tried I, me, through my willpower and my determination, confident that I understand who I am and what's going on, I'm going to fix this. No, no, no, it doesn't work. Both systems recognize that the root of the problem isn't something else out there that I can solve. The root of the problem is not really understanding who we are, what this world is. And so the solution is a change in understanding, not a fix. And that change comes through experience, it comes through commitment,"}, {"t": 379, "text": "it comes through an awful lot of time, a lot of patience. You go through these steps again and again and again, just like we come to practice again and again and again. You might have noticed, I don't know if you noticed this, although there are apparently some sort of stages and steps in practice and stuff like that. We don't have a graduation ceremony. Well, maybe death. There's no graduation."}, {"t": 409, "text": "Because we're always learning, right? We're always students of the way. It changes, but we're always deeply students. And so I thought it might be interesting and hopefully I'm not stealing Sam's thunder. He was planning to do this as an assignment, but I took up an assignment for myself that I want to share with you. But it might be interesting to see if I could take the language that we're using in class for the 12 steps"}, {"t": 441, "text": "and re-express it with the language of the practice that I grew up in and just see what that feels like. And this is a good thing to do in general. I think sometimes things that are received, we give them a certain kind of helpful power. Like if we really hear something, there's a helpfulness to really feeling its power. But also there's a sort of rigidity that can be unhelpful. So it's always good to give yourself permission"}, {"t": 473, "text": "after you've really taken something in as it is to play with it a little bit, you know, to write your own version. We do this with our precepts, which are a big part of our way of doing the 12 steps is through the precepts. We invite people to write their own versions of the Bodhisattva precepts. Make them your own. So here's my attempt at this. See what you think. Step one in the original."}, {"t": 500, "text": "Admitted that we were powerless over alcohol and that our lives had become unmanageable. Admitted that we were powerless over alcohol and our lives had become unmanageable. Maybe admitted the incredible power of ignorance and conditioning. This one I had to kind of... And our lives lived in that way are inherently unsatisfactory and painful. I'm still working on this one."}, {"t": 539, "text": "But to admit the incredible power of ignorance and conditioning that our lives lived in the shadow of that are inherently unsatisfactory and painful. No matter what you do, right? No matter what you do. So both of these drop a lot out, right? They drop out our sureness about who and what we are. I alone can fix this or solve this. They recognize that what we're working with is much bigger than ourselves"}, {"t": 571, "text": "and there's something that we're not getting about it. If we feel powerless in the face of alcohol or lost in a life of constant whirling and twirling, inherently unsatisfactory, never quite getting to what we want, we have to really bring up a lot of humility, don't we? And a lot of letting go. And a lot of making space for something else. So then step two, original."}, {"t": 603, "text": "Come to believe that a power greater than ourselves could restore us to sanity. This one's pretty close. Come to understand that a way greater than ourselves could restore us to sanity. So the way, the power, something beyond this idea of me is needed here. And Juriki was also very generous in telling us his story of the me"}, {"t": 635, "text": "being knocked down again and again and again. Last time I talked about this, I gave him a hard time for how many times he said, and then the hardest thing in my life happened. How could that keep happening? But it did. And it's tricky. You know, we do have this perception of self"}, {"t": 656, "text": "and the things we're learning and doing. And it's not wrong exactly, but it's not right either. And I thought in all of the areas where his story was shared with us and your stories have been lived, it does seem that when there's real change and growth, there's something bigger happening. It's not something you figured out. It's something bigger than that."}, {"t": 685, "text": "So is it a power? Is it a way? Is it Buddha? And the problem with all of this, when we talk about it, is we only have our words and concepts, right, which are very kind of crude. This is subtle territory. So we recognize that we don't understand. We recognize we need something bigger than us."}, {"t": 707, "text": "And then step three, we've got to commit to it. So it's pretty logical actually, the twelve steps, just like practice. Step three in the original, make a decision to turn our will and our life over the care of God as we understand him, my version. Make a decision to turn our will and our life over to Buddha, Dharma, and Sangha."}, {"t": 736, "text": "To the way of awakening, the teachings, the community, much bigger than me, so much bigger than me. And when studying the Buddha, Dharma, and Sangha, and also I'm sure in studying how to turn your life over to care of God, we recognize that we are a part of this. It's not something over there that we will now join. It's a remembering, right? Remembering like returning."}, {"t": 769, "text": "Remembering literally means embodiment, right? Remembering, these are our members, that we are Buddha, that we are God, that we are ancestors. We were reciting and venerating our ancestors this morning in our morning service. So remembering that we have been ancestors all along, that's bigger than me. And so this commitment isn't like this,"}, {"t": 797, "text": "it takes effort, right? It's hard, but it's not the kind of commitment that's me doing something, right? It's also emerging and a letting go, a softening, but with purpose, right, with direction. Okay, so we've recognized the problem is bigger than self. We need to turn towards that vastness, and we've got to commit to it."}, {"t": 829, "text": "Step four is like, well, let's get to work. What does it actually look like on the ground? And the first suggestion in step four, make a searching and fearless moral inventory of ourselves. Maybe study and practice the precepts, to see and understand our skillful and unskillful actions of body, speech, and mind. Study and practice precepts to see and understand"}, {"t": 863, "text": "our skillful and unskillful actions of body, speech, and mind. In both of these, I think it's not just a list, right? It's understanding the being experience that led to all those things happening, right? It's like following a chain of evidence back, you know? It's not our opinions, it's seeing what we actually do and wondering what's happening with this being that does these things."}, {"t": 892, "text": "So you're looking at yourself a little bit from the perspective of God and Buddha, even though it is all you, and you are the one who is responsible for doing something about it. But it's not exactly your fault, right? You got here honestly, through all the forces that formed your conditioned being."}, {"t": 915, "text": "So that's step four. We start getting real about what's happening. And step five is just a continuation of that. Go deeper, go deeper with this. Original language. Admitted to God, to ourselves, being the exact nature of our wrongs. You can't just do this on your own, right?"}, {"t": 935, "text": "No question there. My version. Admitted to Buddha, to our teachers, to ourselves, to trusted others, maybe right here, the exact nature of our confusion, right? Our confusion, our wrongs."}, {"t": 952, "text": "So this is the pure practice of confession, which we do in a generic way, you know, a general way, almost every time we do a ritual, we avow our ancient twisted karma. Maybe there's a little internal effort we should be doing with that kind of practice of, well, what is this really for me? This is avowing my impatience,"}, {"t": 973, "text": "my judgment, right? My narrowness, my righteousness, letting it go. So four and five, I think, are two sides of the same coin, right? Really being real about our conditioned selves. And feeling our conditioned selves"}, {"t": 993, "text": "in the context of the unconditioned, right? In the context of Buddha, in the context of God. Because then there's room, right? Then there's infinite space. And it's only me and mine, and how do I fix this? It's very constricted, right? It's very small. There's no room for anything."}, {"t": 1008, "text": "No wonder we, maybe we seem to succeed for a little while, but then we fall right back on our butts, right? There's no space. And then I don't know what the original original are, but the 12 steps that Juriki gave us, combining some things next, they combine steps six and seven with this statement."}, {"t": 1035, "text": "We're entirely ready to have God remove all these defects of character. Humbly asked him to remove our shortcomings. It doesn't get more bigger in themselves than that. I can't do it. God can do it. But maybe for, in this context, something like, choose to devote ourselves fully to the practice with faith in the way of Buddha,"}, {"t": 1069, "text": "willingness to enter the path of Dharma, humbly devoted to Sangha life. So I'm a little more wordy, I guess. Faith in the way of Buddha, willingness, the path, the teachings of Dharma, humbly devoting ourselves to Sangha life. So this step is almost all like shifting the context, right? We're making a choice, but we're shifting the context."}, {"t": 1098, "text": "It's not just my life anymore, right? God's life, Buddha's life, lived with Dharma and Sangha, lived with God as we understand him or her or them. And then steps eight, nine, and ten are also mixed, combined together. So I'm sure they're just, it just somehow made sense. I don't know what the originals are."}, {"t": 1126, "text": "But eight, nine, and ten that we received from the 12-step tradition, make a list of all persons we have harmed and became willing to make amends to them all, made direct amends to such people wherever possible, except when to do so would injure them or others, continued to take personal inventory, and when we were wrong, promptly admitted it."}, {"t": 1157, "text": "So this is very thorough going. So seeking to right the wrongs, you know? Getting out there, making amends, and being smart about it, right? There's some intelligence in this step, you know? Discerning, sometimes making amends is internal and you don't bother the person anymore. You've caused enough damage."}, {"t": 1181, "text": "You're just setting them up and saying, look, I'm really sorry for what I did back then. Really sorry. Maybe Buddhist language, something like, understood our unskillful actions as violations of precepts, made amends to those who were harmed when possible and appropriate,"}, {"t": 1206, "text": "and continued, continued to practice precepts, with the guidance of teacher and sangha, with humility and dedication. So the second part is really important. It's not a one-time deal, right? This is the practice of a lifetime in both systems. Continued to take personal inventory when we were wrong, admitting it."}, {"t": 1228, "text": "Continuing to practice with precepts. That's one of the things that actually really surprised me and was quite illuminating. Some of the first times I ever met somebody in recovery is that people in recovery, at least I think the way this is understood, they never say they graduated either. They never say, oh, I used to be an addict,"}, {"t": 1249, "text": "but I solved that way back. You know, we have a member who was addicted to alcohol in his 20s, early 30s, who's now close to 80. If you ask him, he'll say, yeah, I'm in recovery. I'm in recovery. They might share how many decades it's been since they've used that substance, but they're still in recovery."}, {"t": 1271, "text": "They're still practicing. So precepts are like that too, right? It's a lifetime thing. So there's a sense of continuity of action, continuity of practice in both of these systems. I think it's so beautiful and so powerful and so important because it's another sort of antidote to our dualistic thinking."}, {"t": 1292, "text": "Like, well, it was if, and here's the problem with thinking something's broken, then you can also mistakenly think that it's fixed, right? And neither is true, not broken or fixed. Almost done here with this. So we've gone this far. Now step 11. Sought through prayer and meditation"}, {"t": 1318, "text": "to improve our conscious contact with God as we understand him, praying only for knowledge of his will and for us, his will for us and the power to carry that out. And this in Christian fashion has capital H for the him and his. Sought through prayer and meditation to improve our conscious contact with God"}, {"t": 1338, "text": "as we understand him, praying only for the knowledge of his will and the power to carry that out. Here, meditation actually gets mentioned, so that's cool. Maybe we might say, practice meditation deeply and continuously"}, {"t": 1357, "text": "to understand our own conditioning. Study the Buddha Dharma to understand the nature of suffering, the causes of suffering, and the path of freedom, right? So the four noble truths for us are God's will, God's will for us, is to practice the path."}, {"t": 1381, "text": "And again, both these systems have this deep, deep acknowledgement, we're not hiding, we can't hide anymore. We're not hiding from our suffering. Even if our suffering is very subtle, you know, very quiet. Maybe I shared this before, but another thing that happened"}, {"t": 1399, "text": "after Juriki shared his life story was somebody came to Dokusan and said, I'm really embarrassed that I have like a really happy parents and great childhood and no real problems and what am I doing here? So we had to explore that a little bit right now. Well, actually,"}, {"t": 1418, "text": "it's different, you know, just different forms for practice. Maybe some are relatively less difficult, I don't know if that's true or not. Anyway, it was interesting. I was a little bit embarrassed, you know, not to be suffering enough. Last step, step 12."}, {"t": 1440, "text": "Having had a spiritual awakening as a result of these steps, we tried to carry this message to alcoholics and to practice these principles in all our affairs. Having had a spiritual awakening as a result of these steps, which could be like, you know,"}, {"t": 1460, "text": "a decade or more of practice, right, as a result of these steps, we tried to carry this message to alcoholics and to practice these principles in all our affairs. So here's the Bodhisattva vision, right, clear as day. Having realized the true nature of being,"}, {"t": 1476, "text": "committed to lives as Bodhisattvas devoted to saving all beings. We have some useful language here that maybe 12-step didn't have access to, like Bodhisattva, being committed to awakening of all beings. And awakening not through, like, cheering ourselves up, right,"}, {"t": 1496, "text": "awakening through deep, deep understanding, wisdom and compassion meeting perfectly. Having realized this, we recognize our commitment, and so it's so beautiful to see this step original, too, that we're not like, oh, thanks, guys, I'm all done now. You know, then you become, I guess,"}, {"t": 1517, "text": "a sponsor and keep going to meetings and support other people and share whenever appropriate how helpful this process could be. So it's the same for us, you know. And we don't run around saying, you know, hey, Zen practice is the best thing ever, you should come immediately, but, you know,"}, {"t": 1537, "text": "if somebody asks, we share, and we're glad if they're interested. Maybe this particular form of practice but it's here and we're open to all because that's our commitment. But one of the things here that can be harder to see in this context is, and I'm sure it's more complicated than this,"}, {"t": 1565, "text": "this would be simplifying, but alcohol can be seen with your eyes, right, and tasted with your tongue and felt in your belly and your mind as it affects you. You can know if you're drinking or not drinking, but our root addiction is not something separate like that."}, {"t": 1587, "text": "Our root illness, our root confusion, our root not-quite-rightness is our very sense of who and what we are, right? Our belief in our separateness, our belief in all that conditioning. It's important to know about that conditioning, but then the trap is we start to really believe it, you know. And then we're limited by it"}, {"t": 1611, "text": "our me-ness, me-ness, our separateness, and how this me is kind of like its own little thing and we've got to spend the rest of our lives protecting it, you know, because it's pretty threatenable, pretty fragile, really. And we have to carry it around all the time."}, {"t": 1635, "text": "And maybe our conditioning is the kind of shadow side of that that we think our me is actually really terrible, really horrible and shameful. So not only we have to carry it around all the time, we have to hide it all the time. What a burden, you know. Pretend to be some better version,"}, {"t": 1652, "text": "better me that other people might like. There's a dishonesty in all of this, right? Like sometimes we talk about the sort of various social conceits, you know, which smooth the wheels and stuff, you know, just kind of being friendly and nice and, hey, how you doing? I don't really mean it,"}, {"t": 1670, "text": "I'm just saying that and blah, blah, blah. But there's a dishonesty in all that, right? That pains us all. So it's tricky, I think, about the path here is it's hard to even see the root of the problem. And I suppose maybe there's more true"}, {"t": 1689, "text": "in 12-step in the exact same way than I understand yet. It does seem like alcohol is a little more apparent than belief in separate self. Because we're so in the middle of ourself, and it's what we operate with"}, {"t": 1705, "text": "and think through and think with that you can't really see yourself, right? The eyeball can't see itself, right? So our practice is more of a felt thing, isn't it? Than a thought thing or a seen thing. Little by little we start to feel that this separateness is a delusion,"}, {"t": 1725, "text": "is an illusion. Maybe sort of vaguely useful in a utility kind of way, but also a prison. And we don't need it. I'm so delighted this morning happened to be the morning that we read, recited Dogen's"}, {"t": 1744, "text": "Genjokoan teaching. So maybe this sounds familiar from that. That Dogen is always whispering in our ears. And there are so many treasures in that, but this one in particular, I think. To study"}, {"t": 1762, "text": "the Buddha way is to study the self. To study the self is to forget the self. To forget the self. That's it, right? To forget the self is to be actualized by myriad things."}, {"t": 1780, "text": "I think the language in our translation is something like that. To forget the self is to be actualized by myriad things. You know, I was thinking about this and the word actualized and myriad are both a little clunky. We don't run around talking about actualized or myriad."}, {"t": 1798, "text": "Maybe something about that, maybe something like to forget the self or to soften our grip on the self is to wake up. To wake up with everything and everyone. That's myriad, right? Everything and everyone."}, {"t": 1817, "text": "Continuously and infinitely. And it all boils down to clinging, right? One way or the other, you know? Clinging to this idea, that idea, and the ideas about who we are are the ones we cling to the most tightly without always even knowing"}, {"t": 1838, "text": "that we are clinging to them because it seems true. I am this kind of person. And in the end, it's not actually so mystical exactly, although everything is mystical. We do this through experience, right? We didn't just read about sesshin,"}, {"t": 1866, "text": "you know? There's some wonderful things about sesshin. Books and essays. Gary Snyder has an early essay of a sesshin he went to at a monastery in Japan. It's just beautiful. Very moving to read that essay. But we are not reading"}, {"t": 1882, "text": "an essay. We are doing sesshin. We are doing the practice. We are experiencing ourselves. Experiencing ourselves. We are experiencing ourselves, experiencing ourselves. And we all have experiences when we are in our own way."}, {"t": 1899, "text": "We all have experiences when we get out of our way. We all have experiences when the clinging to self softens and loosens and we are open. And we all have experiences where we constrict. So that's our true teacher here."}, {"t": 1915, "text": "But luckily, we have teachings to help us navigate. Otherwise, we are just lost in clinging, unclinging, clinging, unclinging, which is all clinging in the end. And Dogen, like all the great Buddhist teachers, is really encouraging us not to settle"}, {"t": 1935, "text": "for feeling a little bit better. Nothing wrong with feeling better but not to settle for that. To have a bigger vision. Twelve steps too, right? That's not just harm reduction. Although harm reduction is an important part of it. It's freedom."}, {"t": 1953, "text": "Like freedom from addiction. To be fully in recovery. To be free. For us. Complete freedom from clinging is the vision. And so I want to talk a little bit about something connected and then I'll close."}, {"t": 1978, "text": "Hopefully I won't go too much past the time we put on schedule. Because I can't help it the way I think and the way many of us think is it sounds like techniques and stuff to remember and stuff to do and go have the sesshin and do these steps"}, {"t": 1997, "text": "and blah blah. I think there's a deeper commonality and a deeper root in all these systems that doesn't get talked about as much. It's implied. And that is the quality of faith or trust. Which isn't exactly"}, {"t": 2017, "text": "tangible or cognitive or sensible even. I want to close by sharing some teachings on faith that are not from the Christian tradition actually but straight from Buddhism. Some of us have studied a little bit about the 8th century"}, {"t": 2039, "text": "Indian Buddhist teacher Shantideva who was a famous poem, the guide to the Bodhisattva life. He also is credited with another work which is much longer work great big thick book in English translation that might be translated as the training anthology"}, {"t": 2056, "text": "of Shantideva. It's kind of like a manual of practice. He wrote an inspiring poem of practice and then like okay here's the manual. Thousands of Sanskrit verses translated into English it's like yeah four or five hundred pages."}, {"t": 2078, "text": "And I haven't gotten that far in it because it's really long but I'm into it. Little by little. And what I really want to share just now is where he starts. He's going to lay out this vast path of practice you know. He thought about this a lot clearly."}, {"t": 2097, "text": "And where does he start? Faith. Faith. Let me just share a little bit from Shantideva. On behalf of yourself and all beings please reflect. First he's going to have a little verse"}, {"t": 2118, "text": "and then he's going to have a long quotation about faith. Here's the verse. This is his verse. Someone who wishes to end suffering and go to the utmost limits of happiness should securely plant the root"}, {"t": 2134, "text": "of faith and fix the mind unwaveringly on awakening. Someone who wishes to end suffering and go to the ultimate limits of happiness should securely plant the root of faith."}, {"t": 2151, "text": "I like that language. Plant the root of faith. Fix the mind unwaveringly on awakening. Then he has a long quotation from a sutra. He quotes like sixty, seventy sutras about this thing. So he was a well read guy. This one's called"}, {"t": 2170, "text": "the Precious Lamp Dharani Sutra which I hadn't heard of before. So there's a little repetition here so just settle into it and I will try to restrain from commenting. Just want to share this vision of faith. One sort of presage I want to make is it does"}, {"t": 2188, "text": "use the language victorious ones to talk about people who've gone far in their practice. Victorious ones. They use the language victory and war a bit in these early teachings. Your defilements are the enemies and you overcome them. You're victorious."}, {"t": 2204, "text": "Just the language they use. Okay, so here's from the Precious Lamp Dharani Sutra. Because of having faith in the victorious ones and their teachings and by having faith in the practice of the Buddha's heirs, because of having faith in the highest"}, {"t": 2221, "text": "awakening, the mind of a great person is born. Faith coming first is the mother, giving birth to all abilities, protecting them, helping them grow. Faith thrives away doubts and carries"}, {"t": 2237, "text": "you over the flood. Faith shows you the city of peace. Faith makes the mind pure and clear. Free from concept and conceit is the root of respect. Faith is the root of respect."}, {"t": 2253, "text": "Faith is the storehouse of wealth, is your best foot. Like a hand is the source of all good karma. Faith leads to joy and giving and brings delight in the teachings of the victorious ones."}, {"t": 2269, "text": "Faith makes you distinguished in abilities and pristine awareness. Shows you the state of a Buddha and helps you attain it. Faith clarifies your faculties and makes them sharper. The power of faith cannot be overcome."}, {"t": 2285, "text": "Is the basis of eliminating the reactive emotions. Faith seeks out your natural abilities for those who have faith are not attached to the pleasures of contact."}, {"t": 2301, "text": "Faith wards off unfavorable circumstances and is itself the single best opportunity. Faith shows you the highest path of freedom. Faith is the unspoiled seed, the cause of abilities."}, {"t": 2323, "text": "Faith grows into the tree of awakening. Increases the special bliss of pristine awareness. Faith shows you all the victorious ones, all the awakened ones. Those who have faith and devotion"}, {"t": 2339, "text": "to the Buddha, don't give up on moral discipline and their training. And those who don't give up on moral discipline and their training are praised as having abilities above all others. Having inconceivable confidence in Dharma, those"}, {"t": 2356, "text": "who have faith and devotion to the spiritual community will have irreversible confidence. And having irreversible confidence becomes the irreversible power of faith. And it goes on like that"}, {"t": 2372, "text": "for quite some time. So let's play with that today. You know, we're so evaluative and more than a little judgy, right? Is this working for me? Am I doing it right? Am I doing it wrong? Is so-and-so doing it right"}, {"t": 2388, "text": "or wrong? Admiring and maybe not so much admiring other people. Do I want to be here? What time is it? How long is this going to go on? Blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. We can just go back to faith, you know? Faith in the breath. Faith in this moment."}, {"t": 2404, "text": "Faith in sunlight. Faith in each other. Faith in practice. Faith that although it is really true that we are deeply conditioned in a million, zillion ways. All kind of addictive stuff"}, {"t": 2421, "text": "going on. Faith that we are also free. Faith that we are also Buddha. Faith that we are also God. Tension, the equally extent."}], "matched": 0.997};
